module gunbc.guarantee_stall.signed_and_ratio_measure_carrier_stall

import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.guarantee_stall { GuaranteeStall, AwaitsOneGrounding, BoundedPopulation, climbs_when }

// WHY THIS ROW EXISTS RATHER THAN A FOURTH REVIEW ROUND. Three reviews in succession flagged the
// unit-modeling class across gunbc.spark.serving_critical_path. Most of it was a real fork and is
// now migrated: started_micros, elapsed_micros, four inter-iteration millis, wall_micros across
// twenty-four sample rows, and two watt fields all consume std.measure.
//
// Three fields did not migrate, and leaving them as bare Int with no row is precisely what the
// third review objected to -- not the Int, but the SILENCE. DESIGN 4b(2) says a class below its
// ceiling must name its next-rung trigger, and 4b(3) says a rung may be lowered only by declaring
// it. An undeclared Int beside a migrated Microsecond in the same record is an unstated divergence.
//
// WHAT ACTUALLY BLOCKS EACH ONE. Three different carrier gaps, not one:
//
//   intercept_micros             a fit intercept, computed by division, may be negative
//   intercept_difference_micros  a difference of two intercepts, signed by construction
//   micros_per_thousand_tokens   a RATE: microseconds per token
//   milli_tokens_per_second      a rate at MILLI scale, in gunbc.spark.serving_step_cost
//   gigabits_per_second_tenths   a link throughput reading
//   link_gigabits                the link's rated throughput
//
// SIGNED. Microsecond is Measure<Time, Micro, Nat>, Nat-backed, so it cannot hold either of the two
// signed values, and std.measure declares no signed duration alias.
//
// QUOTIENT. micros_per_thousand_tokens is a dimensional quotient. std.measure publishes
// measure_add, measure_le, measure_count and two scale-fraction folds -- no product, no quotient,
// and no composition on the Quantity type -- so a microseconds-per-token carrier cannot be spelled.
//
// SCALE. milli_tokens_per_second is a rate at milli scale. TokensPerSecond is Measure<Frequency,
// One, Nat>, and its module states the reason the milli scaling exists: rounding a sub-unit rate to
// an integer would report zero. Migrating to the One-scaled alias would erase the distinction the
// field was created to preserve, so this needs Measure<Frequency, Milli, Nat> and no alias exists.
//
// AND ONE IS BLOCKED BY A FORK RATHER THAN AN ABSENCE, which is the worst of the four. The two RoCE
// fields would take std.measure Bandwidth = Measure<DataRate, One, Nat>, and std.measure's own
// comment beside MegatransfersPerSecond declares DataRate to be bits/s. Its live consumers do not
// agree with that or with each other: extdeps.systems.nvidia records the GB10's 273 GB/s memory
// bandwidth as bandwidth(count: 273000000000), which is bytes per second, while extdeps.colo
// natcoweb records colo transit ports as bandwidth(count: 1000000) and bandwidth(count: 10000000),
// which are neither. Three consumers, at least two mutually inconsistent readings of one carrier's
// base unit. Joining that carrier would make these rows a THIRD reading and cement the fork, so they
// stay declared here until the base unit has one answer.
data signed_and_ratio_measure_carrier_stall: GuaranteeStall = GuaranteeStall {
  subject: "unit fields across gunbc.spark.serving_critical_path and gunbc.spark.serving_step_cost carry bare Nat or Int beside migrated Watt, Hertz, Percent, Milliwatt and Microsecond fields in the same records, because std.measure has no signed duration alias, no dimensional quotient, no milli-scaled frequency alias, and no agreed base unit for Bandwidth",
  current: Mitigatable,
  ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
  blocker: AwaitsOneGrounding { grounding: "std.measure" },
  population: BoundedPopulation { members: ["gunbc.spark.serving_critical_path", "gunbc.spark.serving_step_cost"] },
  next_rung_trigger: climbs_when(capability: "std.measure carries a signed duration magnitude, a dimensional quotient with the arithmetic to inhabit it, a milli-scaled frequency alias, and one adjudicated base unit for DataRate that every live Bandwidth consumer is migrated onto, sufficient to express a fit intercept computed by division, a signed difference of two intercepts, a microseconds-per-token rate, a sub-unit token rate, and a link throughput without any consumer unwrapping to a bare scalar to do so")
}
